    "field": "Limnology",
    "problem": "The paper addresses the limited understanding of how deep-water temperatures in large lakes respond to climate change, a gap caused by the scarcity of detailed, long-term subsurface observations compared to the abundance of less-representative surface measurements.",
    "method": "This study analyzes a unique 30-year, high-frequency subsurface temperature dataset from Lake Michigan using statistical trend decomposition to quantify deep-water warming and link it to observed shifts in the timing of seasonal overturn, the winter cooling period, and the onset of summer stratification.",
    "task": "The task is to analyze three decades of high-frequency subsurface water temperature data from Lake Michigan to demonstrate how changes in seasonal overturn and stratification, driven by climate change, cause deep-water warming in large lakes.",
